    description: "C# user-typed property/member symbols are misnamed with their TYPE, not the member name.\n\nBehavioral evidence (DEEP cohort deep-20260623-134237, sherpa-csharp; live analyze_csharp probe): a C# property whose declared return type is a user-defined type is emitted under the TYPE name, not the member name — `public WaveInfo Info => _info;` emits symbol `C.WaveInfo` (should be `C.Info`); `public FeatureConfig Feat { get; set; }` emits `C.FeatureConfig`. Two same-typed properties collapse to one entry in symbol_by_name (name-index collision). Predefined/array/generic return types are non-identifier AST nodes, so only USER-typed properties misfired — which masked the bug and made a name-based property search return 0 (the symptom INV-dakit's filing misattributed to non-emission).\n\nRoot cause: `extract_name_from_declaration` (csharp.py) grabbed the first `identifier` child, but in a C# `property_declaration` a user-typed return type is itself an `identifier` PRECEDING the property-name identifier. Fix: read the grammar's `name` field instead. Verified no-op for class/interface/struct/enum/constructor (their name field == the first identifier). Methods were unaffected (separate type-aware extraction path).\n\nDiscovered while investigating INV-dakit (whose filed 'expression-bodied not emitted' mechanism was empirically false — emission always worked). Sibling of INV-dakit."  # 7329
    fields: {}  # 7329
